program.py\nresults/ideas/03_log_odds_shift_residual_decoder/program_1/src/invariant_checks.py\nresults/ideas/03_log_odds_shift_residual_decoder/program_1/src/surrogate.py\n{\n  \"combined_score\": 0.9453539826294075,\n  \"correct\": true,\n  \"entrypoint_path\": \"src/program.py\",\n  \"program_dir\": \"work/results/t07/codex/20261117_111440/investigation/ideas/03_log_odds_shift_residual_decoder/program_1\",\n  \"source_attempt_id\": \"cycle_03_log_odds_shift_residual_decoder_attempt_01\",\n  \"updated_at\": \"2026-11-17T13:21:48.668907+00:00\"\n}[\n  \"campaign_results_dir\",\n  \"count\",\n  \"generated_at\",\n  \"queries\",\n  \"reference_query_plan\",\n  \"references\",\n  \"results_dir\"\n]\n@Article{Gaylor1965DesignFO,\n title = {Design for Optimal Prediction in Simple Linear Regression},\n@Article{Mahmoud2025SynergizingML,\n title = {Synergizing machine learning and experimental analysis to predict post‐heating compressive strength in waste concrete},\n@Article{Derezinski2019BayesianED,\n title = {Bayesian experimental design using regularized determinantal point processes},\n@Article{Jakhar2023MachineLP,\n title = {Machine learning predictive models for optimal design of photovoltaic/thermal collector with nanofluids based geothermal cooling},\n@Article{Chen2022FatigueFA,\n title = {Fatigue Factor Assessment and Life Prediction of Concrete Based on Bayesian Regularized BP Neural Network},\n@Article{Amor2021PredictionOF,\n title = {Prediction of functional properties of nano TiO2\\documentclass[12pt]{minimal} \\usepackage{amsmath} \\usepackage{wasysym} \\usepackage{amsfonts} \\usepackage{amssymb} \\usepackage{amsbsy} \\usepackage{mathrsfs} \\usepackage{upgreek} \\setlength{\\oddsidemargin}{-69pt} \\begin{document}$$\\hbox {TiO}_2$$\\end{do},\n@Article{Ravi2016ExperimentalDO,\n title = {Experimental Design on a Budget for Sparse Linear Models and Applications},\n@Article{Zhang2012PredictionOX,\n title = {Prediction of xylanase optimal temperature by support vector regression},\n@Article{Gültekin2026PredictionOT,\n title = {Prediction of tensile strength of PLA parts produced with FDM using Taguchi L27, ANOVA, and large-scale machine learning models and optimal parameter design},\n@Article{Jackson2018OnTG,\n title = {On the generalizability of linear and non-linear region of interest-based multivariate regression models for fMRI data},\n@Article{Neuberger2024GoalOO,\n title = {Goal Oriented Optimal Design of Infinite-Dimensional Bayesian Inverse Problems using Quadratic Approximations},\n@Article{Yakut2025OptimizingWT,\n title = {Optimizing wastewater treatment: artificial intelligence-based prediction and green synthesis of iron nanoparticles for efficient dye removal},\n@Article{Yan2025PredictionOC,\n title = {Prediction of Compressive Strength of Carbon Nanotube Reinforced Concrete Based on Multi-Dimensional Database},\n@Article{Qu2025AnES,\n title = {An Efficient Sparse Twin Parametric Insensitive Support Vector Regression Model},\n@Inproceedings{Fisher2012OptimalAE,\n title = {Optimal and efficient experimental design for nonparametric regression with application to functional data},\n@Article{Gladys2026OptimizationAP,\n title = {Optimization and predictive modelling of mechanical characteristics of 3D printed olefin block copolymer polyethylene: RSM and machine learning methods},\n@Article{Sagnol2023FastSR,\n title = {Fast Screening Rules for Optimal Design via Quadratic Lasso Reformulation},\n@Inproceedings{Neuberger2026PathOEDFI,\n title = {Path-OED for infinite-dimensional Bayesian linear inverse problems governed by PDEs},\n@Article{Han2026DesignOC,\n title = {Design of copper-based mixed metal oxygen reduction electrocatalyst driven by large-scale computing simulation and experimental data},\n@Article{May2026OptimalDO,\n title = {Optimal design of experiments for functional linear models with dynamic factors},\n@Inproceedings{Nguyen2015GaussianFR,\n title = {Gaussian functional regression for state estimation with linear partial diﬀerential equations},\n@Article{Aljomah2026IntegratingEP,\n title = {Integrating Experimental Pyrolysis and Machine Learning for Sustainable Biochar Yield Prediction from Lignocellulosic Waste},\n@Article{Uto2020AGM,\n title = {A generalized many-facet Rasch model and its Bayesian estimation using Hamiltonian Monte Carlo},\n@Article{Henning1988TheIO,\n title = {The influence of test and sample dimensionality on latent trait person ability and item difficulty calibrations},\n@Article{Robitzsch2021ACS,\n title = {A Comprehensive Simulation Study of Estimation Methods for the Rasch Model},\nfinal\nrebuild.py\nsearch\n# Log-odds shift residual decoder\n\nThe unchanged score-targeted all-query ridge described below remains the\nacquisition method and exact zero-nonlinear-weight fallback. The added decoder,\nits nested validation, resource bounds and untested status are documented in\n`DECODER.md`. `program.py` delegates fitting and prediction to\n`nonlinear_decoder.py`; `surrogate.py` and `invariant_checks.py` are unchanged.\n\nThe starter's `handle(request)` operations, transport, upload bounds, response\nkeys and cumulative-observation protocol are preserved. Only source rows and\ntheir supplied anonymous groups/weights train the method. No project code,\ntests, evaluation, network requests or experiments were run in this editing\nstage. There are no measured performance or improvement claims.\n\n## Source model and nested acquisition\n\n`surrogate.fit_score_model` normalizes weights in every fit, computes weighted\nitem means and source full-row means, then forms weighted centered responses\nand their covariance with the full-row score. Full-row means retain all\noriginal item columns and multiplicities. Canonical source-row ordering makes\narithmetic insensitive to permutations of the uploaded source rows/weights.\n\n`select_score_path` greedily maximizes squared conditional score covariance\ndivided by regularized conditional item variance. A positive ridge enters both\nselected covariance and candidate variance. Empty conditioning is explicit.\nEach selected item masks its entire content group; exact ties use lowest item\nindex. The design is completed at fit time and never sees target answers.\nEvery select returns a cumulative prefix, including an empty prefix at zero.\nConstant sources have zero gains and deterministic legal selections, without\nswitching algorithms based on control observations.\n\nOnly source-by-item responses, item-by-selected cross-covariances, and at most\n25-by-25 selected covariance matrices are formed. Cross-covariance columns\nare cached across ridge paths within each fold. There is no item-by-item\ncovariance or reconstruction of unobserved target answers. Fold models are\nprocessed sequentially, and the final cross-column cache is released.\n\n## Incumbent decoder and inner validation\n\n`predict_score` uses all cumulative answers in direct regularized prediction of\nthe full score. The optional offset prediction is source score mean plus mean\ndifficulty-centered observed response. A source-selected convex blend is\nclipped to [0,1]. Zero observations return the source prior before computing\nan empty mean. An actual census returns its observed exact score. This is a\nmodel-based estimator, not a design-unbiased audit estimator.\n\nSource validation uses two disjoint held-out partitions of shuffled supplied\nlineage IDs (alternating positions), together covering all supplied groups.\nEach fold refits item means, score covariances, and the entire greedy path;\nno validation row participates in its own design. For each setting, absolute\nerrors are averaged equally over declared prefixes, then within each lineage\nusing renormalized supplied weights, then equally over held-out lineages.\nPrefixes are unique clipped values of {5,10,25} plus maximum budget; for maximum\nbudgets up to five they are clipped {2,5}. Neither actual target observations\nnor target scores select prefixes, hyperparameters or items.\n\nThe deterministic setting list orders blend weights {1,0.5,0}, and within\neach blend ridge strengths {0.05,0.01,0.2}. `cv_settings` caps the number of\nsettings from this list. `training_steps` caps complete selector designs,\nreserving one for deployment and allowing two fold paths per distinct ridge;\nfold statistics and paths are reused across blend weights. These allowances\nare advisory in the RPC; this closed-form method has no optimizer iterations.\nIf allowances cannot support two fold paths, fewer than three source groups\nexist, weights vanish in a required partition, or maximum budget is zero, the\nfixed fallback is ridge 0.05 and blend 1 (pure score regression). Winning\nsettings and the final path are frozen at fit, including across later budgets.\n\n## Executable safeguards and limitations\n\n`invariant_checks.py` is imported by production code: selection checks enforce\ncontent uniqueness and prefix extension; prediction checks enforce bounded,\nfinite outputs and identical-history equality. Predictions decode sorted unique\nhistories once and scatter back, so duplicate histories are exactly equal and\ntarget-row permutations cannot change reduction order. Fits use\none BLAS thread and reset/close discard all episode state.\n\nAnonymous supplied lineage groups are not complete ancestry metadata. The\nprotocol cannot implement per-target adaptive acquisition, request extra\nlabels, evaluate IRT/anchor baselines, or produce empirical paper tables in\nthis stage. Comparing those methods, uncertainty analysis, ablations and the\nofficial-template research paper remain unperformed, not claimed completed.\nNo forbidden literature or external research materials were accessed.\n# Candidate: log-odds shift plus residual ridge\n\n## Interface and unchanged acquisition\n\nOnly `program.py:handle` is public. The starter's operations, upload limits,\ncumulative histories, transitions and response schemas are unchanged. In\nparticular, update returns only `{\"ok\": true}`. No additional observations,\nmetadata, target scores or target-specific acquisitions are assumed.\n\n`nonlinear_decoder.fit_candidate` first fits the original incumbent on the\noriginal supplied source arrays, using the original seed offset and tuning\nallowances. It calls the unchanged `surrogate.py` tuning, fitting, selection and\nprediction functions. Its full-source selection order is frozen before any\nnonlinear decoder tuning and remains the common nested path for all targets.\nEach item content group can be selected only once. A zero nonlinear weight\nuses the incumbent prediction directly, without evaluating the nonlinear path.\n\n## Prefix-specific nonlinear model\n\nWeighted source item means are clipped to [0.0001, 0.9999] and transformed into\nlogits. For each response row at a queried prefix, infer a single shift by\nminimizing summed Bernoulli negative log likelihood plus half the shift penalty\ntimes squared shift. Safeguarded Newton iterations use a [-20,20] bracket and\nat most 64 iterations. Converged rows freeze independently, so adding other\ntarget histories cannot change a row's stopping iteration. Positive penalties\ngive finite estimates for all-zero and all-one histories.\n\nThe score curve is the equal-item mean of shifted item probabilities over\n**every original item column**. Probability matrices are evaluated in chunks\nof 1024 item columns. Repeated-content columns retain their original full-score\nmultiplicity; content deduplication affects queries only.\n\nFor each queried prefix and shift penalty, source shifts are inferred from\nthat prefix's binary answers only, never from their full scores. A source row's\nfull score minus its curve score is the regression label; its queried answers\nminus its shifted query probabilities are the features. Weighted centering\nand a positive ridge fit all features, with an unpenalized intercept. Target\npredictions are the curve plus intercept plus residual features times fitted\ncoefficients, blended with the incumbent and finally clipped to [0,1].\nThe curve-only configuration disables both the residual intercept and residual\ncoefficients. Source prefix statistics are reused across residual ridge and\nblend choices. Deployment caches only fitted source coefficients by prefix.\n\n## Source-only selection and allowances\n\nThe deterministic shortlist, in tie-breaking order, is:\n\n1. Unchanged incumbent (nonlinear weight zero).\n2. Penalty 1, residual ridge 0.05, weights 0.5 then 1.\n3. Penalty 0.1, residual ridge 0.05, weights 0.5 then 1.\n4. Penalty 1, residual ridge 0.01, weights 0.5 then 1.\n5. Penalty 1, curve-only, weights 0.5 then 1.\n\n`cv_settings` caps this list at nine entries, with the incumbent always\navailable. No target observations, number/order of targets, control patterns\nor seed identity choose a decoder. Seeds only shuffle source lineage folds.\n\nAt least six source lineage groups are required for nonlinear validation.\nTwo disjoint outer held-out lineage partitions together cover every group.\nEach outer training partition refits the full incumbent preprocessing, inner\nlineage validation and greedy paths before fitting its nonlinear decoder.\nOuter held-out rows never participate in their own item means, path tuning,\nselection, source ability/residual fitting or regression labels. Their shifts\nuse only their allowed queried responses. Inner folds cannot see outer held-out\ngroups. No all-source path is reused in an outer validation fold.\n\nAll shortlisted decoders are compared on the same fold-local query prefixes.\nAbsolute errors are averaged equally over the incumbent's declared supported\nprefixes, then within each held-out lineage using normalized supplied weights,\nthen equally over held-out lineages. One setting is selected for all budgets;\nties retain the earlier setting, including the incumbent. These validation\nlosses are internal tuning statistics, not reported experimental results.\n\nThe original full-source incumbent keeps its original allowances. Its design\ncost is bounded by one final path plus two paths per distinct tuned ridge\n(at most seven). Remaining `training_steps` allow two outer incumbent fits;\neach gets at most seven paths and must have at least three to support inner\nvalidation. Consequently, outer fits may use fewer inner ridge candidates\nthan the full-source incumbent. Fold-local blend tuning is still performed.\nThe total selector path bound is the deployment cost plus twice the allocated\nfold cost, never exceeding the supplied allowance when validation is enabled.\nAs in the starter, an unconditional fallback deployment still needs one path\neven if the advisory allowance is zero. There is no iterative model optimizer.\n\nIf source groups, positive partition weights, settings or design allowances\nare insufficient, the unchanged incumbent wins without target-dependent\nswitching. After selection, the nonlinear model is refitted on all sources\nfor each requested prefix. Nonlinear-only source arrays are canonically sorted\nby response bytes, weight and group before fitting. Every target history is\ndecoded independently; sorted unique histories are decoded once then scattered\nback. With zero queried items the exact incumbent weighted source score prior\nis returned. An actual census retains the starter's exact observed-score path.\nThe original imported runtime invariants check nested content uniqueness,\nfinite bounded predictions and identical-history equality. New production\nchecks enforce group separation, prefix membership and finite shifts/losses.\n\n## Status, files and limitations\n\nChanged `program.py` and `IMPLEMENTATION.md`; added `nonlinear_decoder.py` and\nthis document. `program_spec.toml`, `surrogate.py` and `invariant_checks.py`\nremain unchanged. No dependencies were added.\n\nThis editing stage prohibits candidate execution, tests, benchmarks, evaluator\ninspection, network access and external literature search. None were performed.\nNo runtime, accuracy, ranking, uncertainty estimate or improvement is claimed.\nThe nonlinear hypothesis remains untested; the harness owns the subsequent\nofficial evaluation. No empirical baseline comparison, ablation experiment or\npaper has been produced. No restricted research material was accessed.\n\nAnonymous lineage IDs cannot establish complete ancestry separation. Nested\nvalidation can be noisy with few lineages, and its selected setting can\noverfit the shortlist. Source-only validation is not a guarantee under target\ndistribution shift. The scalar curve assumes a common log-odds shift; residual\nridge can still extrapolate poorly. The method is model-based, not a\ndesign-unbiased audit estimator. The interface cannot request per-target\nadaptive items, external benchmark labels, or empirical baseline evaluations.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
